// ERC4626Min — tokenised vault, route layer.
//
// The full ERC-4626 route set over the textbook proportional conversion. What
// this entity claims is the shape of the interface: who may call each route,
// what each route reverts on, and which way each inexact division rounds. It
// does not claim that any particular number comes out, and it is not safe to
// deploy against an untrusted first depositor. `ERC4626.cam` is that vault.
//
// Ground truth: `product/stdlib/bls/erc4626-min.md`. Read §7 of that document
// before choosing this over the full vault — the first-holder attack is
// explicitly out of its scope, and out of scope means "this entity is
// vulnerable to it", not "this entity is fine".
//
// ## What it implements
//
//   - ERC-4626: deposit, mint, withdraw, redeem; asset, totalAssets, the four
//     convert/preview pairs and the four limit routes.
//   - The share as an ERC-20: transfer, transferFrom, approve, balanceOf,
//     allowance, totalSupply, name, symbol, decimals.
//
// ## Rounding, which is the whole safety story here
//
// Every one of the four write routes moves value across the boundary of the
// vault, and every conversion at that boundary can leave a remainder. A
// remainder that resolves toward the caller is value leaving against no
// share, and every other holder pays it. So each direction is fixed the way
// the standard fixes it — against the caller, in favour of the vault:
//
//   deposit / previewDeposit    assets → shares, down
//   mint    / previewMint       shares → assets, up
//   withdraw/ previewWithdraw   assets → shares, up
//   redeem  / previewRedeem     shares → assets, down
//
// `convertToShares` and `convertToAssets` both round down; the standard says
// they are the unbiased informational pair and must not be used to price a
// call.
//
// ## Throw codes
//
//   1  zero amount (share transfer)
//   2  insufficient share balance (share transfer)
//   3  insufficient share allowance (share transferFrom)
//   7  zero address as a share recipient or approval spender
//   20 zero address as a vault receiver or owner
//   21 the owner holds fewer shares than the burn requires
//   22 the caller is neither the owner nor approved for that many shares
//   23 the vault holds less than the asset amount requested
//   24 the asset's transfer answered false
//
// Codes 1–7 are the base token's and mean the same thing here, because the
// share ledger is that token's ledger. 20–24 are the vault's own.
//
// 24 is worth a sentence. ERC-20 lets a token report failure by answering
// `false` instead of reverting, and a vault that ignores the answer credits
// shares against assets that never arrived. So each of the four writes
// captures the boolean and checks it in the following phase. On the way out
// (`withdraw`, `redeem`) the check is after the burn has already committed,
// which is deliberate: the revert unwinds the burn with it, and doing it the
// other way round would mean calling out before the effect.
//
// ## Deviations worth knowing before you deploy this
//
//   - **The first-holder attack works.** The conversion has an empty-vault
//     branch, so the first depositor sets the rate; a one-wei deposit
//     followed by a large direct transfer makes the next depositor's shares
//     round to zero. Use `ERC4626.cam` unless the first deposit is yours.
//   - **The requested amount is credited, not the received amount.** A
//     fee-on-transfer asset delivers less than `assets`, and this vault issues
//     shares against `assets` anyway, so existing holders pay the fee.
//     `ERC4626.cam` reads its own balance either side of the transfer and
//     credits the difference.
//   - **No permit on the share.** EIP-2612 is on the base token and not here;
//     a holder approves a router with `approve`.
//   - **No `supportsInterface`.** ERC-4626 does not require ERC-165, so
//     unlike the uRWA entities this is not a missed MUST.
//
// None of the three is a defect to patch locally: the first two are what
// separates this entity from the full one, and the third is what the standard
// asks for.

import "./erc4626-core.cam"

entity ERC4626Min {

    // ERC-4626 names both, and an indexer needs them: a vault's deposit
    // history is not recoverable from the share ledger, because `Transfer`
    // from the zero address says how many shares appeared and nothing about
    // what was paid for them. The share token owes the ERC-20 log as well.
    event Deposit(indexed sender: address, indexed owner: address,
                  assets: U256, shares: U256);
    event Withdraw(indexed sender: address, indexed receiver: address,
                   indexed owner: address, assets: U256, shares: U256);
    event Transfer(indexed from_addr: address, indexed to: address, value: U256);
    event Approval(indexed owner: address, indexed spender: address, value: U256);

    // The asset is an identity, not a constructor argument, for the same
    // reason `UniswapV2Pair`'s two tokens are: it participates in the CREATE2
    // salt, so a caller can compute the vault's address for a given asset
    // before the vault exists. It is also what makes the member typed, which
    // is what lets `balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset` lower to a call.
    identity m_asset: Address<VaultAsset>

    routes {
        #[factory_only]
        constructor(name_: String, symbol_: String, decimals_: u8) => []

        // ── ERC-4626 writes ─────────────────────────────────────────────
        //
        // All four are phased, and they have to be: the conversion needs the
        // vault's asset balance, which is a cross-contract read, and a `~>`
        // capture is only readable from a later phase. The route-level
        // `where` therefore carries only what can be decided without that
        // read, and the amount checks sit on a per-phase `where`.
        //
        // The `let` bound in `read:` and consumed in the settling phase is
        // what makes the answer the pre-commit one. A `return` runs after the
        // member transforms have committed, so recomputing the conversion
        // there would divide by the supply the call had just changed.

        deposit(assets: U256, receiver: address) -> U256
            where receiver != zero_address() : throw 20
        => [
            read: [
                var held = balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset;
                let issued = ratio_shares_down(assets, m_total_supply, held);
            ]
            pull: [
                var paid = transferFrom(msg::sender, sys::address, assets) ~> m_asset;
            ]
            issue where paid : throw 24 : [
                emit Transfer(zero_address(), receiver, issued);
                emit Deposit(msg::sender, receiver, assets, issued);
                return(issued)
            ]
        ]

        // `mint` is the same trade read from the other end: the caller names
        // the shares and pays whatever they cost, rounded up.
        mint(shares: U256, receiver: address) -> U256
            where receiver != zero_address() : throw 20
        => [
            read: [
                var held = balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset;
                let cost = ratio_assets_up(shares, m_total_supply, held);
            ]
            pull: [
                var paid = transferFrom(msg::sender, sys::address, cost) ~> m_asset;
            ]
            issue where paid : throw 24 : [
                emit Transfer(zero_address(), receiver, shares);
                emit Deposit(msg::sender, receiver, cost, shares);
                return(cost)
            ]
        ]

        // The asset leaves in the same phase the shares are burnt, and the
        // burn commits first: member transforms are written before the phase's
        // actions run, so this is checks-effects-interactions without having
        // to say so.
        withdraw(assets: U256, receiver: address, owner: address) -> U256
            where receiver != zero_address() : throw 20 &&
                  owner    != zero_address() : throw 20
        => [
            read: [
                var held = balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset;
                let burnt = ratio_shares_up(assets, m_total_supply, held);
            ]
            settle where held >= assets : throw 23 &&
                        balance_of(m_balances, owner) >= burnt : throw 21 &&
                        share_spend_allowed(m_allowances, owner,
                                            msg::sender, burnt) : throw 22 : [
                var sent = transfer(receiver, assets) ~> m_asset;
            ]
            done where sent : throw 24 : [
                emit Transfer(owner, zero_address(), burnt);
                emit Withdraw(msg::sender, receiver, owner, assets, burnt);
                return(burnt)
            ]
        ]

        redeem(shares: U256, receiver: address, owner: address) -> U256
            where receiver != zero_address() : throw 20 &&
                  owner    != zero_address() : throw 20
        => [
            read: [
                var held = balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset;
                let payout = ratio_assets_down(shares, m_total_supply, held);
            ]
            settle where balance_of(m_balances, owner) >= shares : throw 21 &&
                        share_spend_allowed(m_allowances, owner,
                                            msg::sender, shares) : throw 22 : [
                var sent = transfer(receiver, payout) ~> m_asset;
            ]
            done where sent : throw 24 : [
                emit Transfer(owner, zero_address(), shares);
                emit Withdraw(msg::sender, receiver, owner, payout, shares);
                return(payout)
            ]
        ]

        // ── ERC-4626 reads ──────────────────────────────────────────────

        view asset() -> address => [ return(m_asset) ]

        // The vault's own balance on the asset, not a running total it keeps
        // itself. A tracked counter would be cheaper and would be wrong about
        // every asset the vault does not control: a direct transfer, a fee, a
        // rebase and a seizure all move the real number and none of them call
        // a route. What a holder can redeem is decided by what is actually
        // there.
        view totalAssets() -> U256 => [
            read: [ var held = balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset; ]
            out:  [ return(held) ]
        ]

        // The informational pair. Both round down, and neither is what a
        // caller should price a trade against — that is what the four
        // `preview` routes are for, and they disagree with these two by one
        // unit exactly when the division is inexact.
        view convertToShares(assets: U256) -> U256 => [
            read: [ var held = balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset; ]
            out:  [ return(ratio_shares_down(assets, m_total_supply, held)) ]
        ]

        view convertToAssets(shares: U256) -> U256 => [
            read: [ var held = balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset; ]
            out:  [ return(ratio_assets_down(shares, m_total_supply, held)) ]
        ]

        // Each `preview` answers exactly what the matching write route
        // returns at the same state — that is P0-9, and it is the property
        // that makes the four of them worth having at all. They are written
        // as the same call to the same conversion so the two cannot drift.
        view previewDeposit(assets: U256) -> U256 => [
            read: [ var held = balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset; ]
            out:  [ return(ratio_shares_down(assets, m_total_supply, held)) ]
        ]

        view previewMint(shares: U256) -> U256 => [
            read: [ var held = balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset; ]
            out:  [ return(ratio_assets_up(shares, m_total_supply, held)) ]
        ]

        view previewWithdraw(assets: U256) -> U256 => [
            read: [ var held = balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset; ]
            out:  [ return(ratio_shares_up(assets, m_total_supply, held)) ]
        ]

        view previewRedeem(shares: U256) -> U256 => [
            read: [ var held = balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset; ]
            out:  [ return(ratio_assets_down(shares, m_total_supply, held)) ]
        ]

        // The deposit side is uncapped, so the limit is the type's ceiling.
        // The withdrawal side is not: it is what the owner's shares are
        // actually worth, which makes P0-10 ("above the limit reverts") a
        // claim with content rather than a vacuous truth about 2^256.
        view maxDeposit(receiver: address) -> U256 => [
            return(u256_max())
        ]

        view maxMint(receiver: address) -> U256 => [
            return(u256_max())
        ]

        view maxWithdraw(owner: address) -> U256 => [
            read: [ var held = balanceOf(sys::address) ~> m_asset; ]
            out:  [
                return(ratio_assets_down(balance_of(m_balances, owner),
                                         m_total_supply, held))
            ]
        ]

        view maxRedeem(owner: address) -> U256 => [
            return(balance_of(m_balances, owner))
        ]

        // ── The share, as an ERC-20 ─────────────────────────────────────
        //
        // Same guards, same throw codes and same self-transfer handling as
        // `token/ERC20.cam`; see that file for why the aliasing branch in the
        // balance transform is load-bearing. There is no mint or burn route:
        // shares are created and destroyed by the four vault routes and by
        // nothing else, which is what keeps `totalSupply` meaning "claims
        // against `totalAssets`".
        transfer(to: address, amount: U256) -> bool
            where amount > 0 : throw 1 &&
                  to != zero_address() : throw 7 &&
                  balance_of(m_balances, msg::sender) >= amount : throw 2
        => [
            emit Transfer(msg::sender, to, amount);
            return(true)
        ]

        transferFrom(from_addr: address, to: address, amount: U256) -> bool
            where amount > 0 : throw 1 &&
                  to != zero_address() : throw 7 &&
                  balance_of(m_balances, from_addr) >= amount : throw 2 &&
                  allowance_of(m_allowances, from_addr, msg::sender) >= amount : throw 3
        => [
            emit Transfer(from_addr, to, amount);
            return(true)
        ]

        approve(spender: address, amount: U256) -> bool
            where spender != zero_address() : throw 7
        => [
            emit Approval(msg::sender, spender, amount);
            return(true)
        ]

        view name() -> String => [ return(m_name) ]
        view symbol() -> String => [ return(m_symbol) ]
        view decimals() -> u8 => [ return(m_decimals) ]
        view totalSupply() -> U256 => [ return(m_total_supply) ]

        view balanceOf(owner: address) -> U256 => [
            return(balance_of(m_balances, owner))
        ]

        view allowance(owner: address, spender: address) -> U256 => [
            return(allowance_of(m_allowances, owner, spender))
        ]
    }

    m_name: String { in constructor(name_, _, _) => name_ }
    m_symbol: String { in constructor(_, symbol_, _) => symbol_ }
    m_decimals: u8 { in constructor(_, _, decimals_) => decimals_ }

    // Every transform below recomputes its conversion from `held` and the
    // pre-commit `m_total_supply` rather than reading the `let` the route
    // bound. A member transform cannot see a route-body binding — they are
    // separate scopes — and the recomputation is exact rather than merely
    // close: transforms in one phase all read the state as it was when the
    // phase began, so `m_total_supply` here is the same value the route's
    // `read:` phase saw.
    m_total_supply: U256 {
        in deposit(assets, _) => issue: {
            m_total_supply + ratio_shares_down(assets, m_total_supply, held)
        }
        in mint(shares, _) => issue: { m_total_supply + shares }
        in withdraw(assets, _, _) => settle: {
            m_total_supply - ratio_shares_up(assets, m_total_supply, held)
        }
        in redeem(shares, _, _) => settle: { m_total_supply - shares }
    }

    m_balances: HashMap<address, U256> {
        in deposit(assets, receiver) => issue: {
            let prev = balance_of(m_balances, receiver);
            m_balances.update(receiver,
                prev + ratio_shares_down(assets, m_total_supply, held))
        }
        in mint(shares, receiver) => issue: {
            let prev = balance_of(m_balances, receiver);
            m_balances.update(receiver, prev + shares)
        }
        in withdraw(assets, _, owner) => settle: {
            let prev = balance_of(m_balances, owner);
            m_balances.update(owner,
                prev - ratio_shares_up(assets, m_total_supply, held))
        }
        in redeem(shares, _, owner) => settle: {
            let prev = balance_of(m_balances, owner);
            m_balances.update(owner, prev - shares)
        }
        in transfer(to, amount) => {
            let bal_from = balance_of(m_balances, msg::sender);
            let bal_to   = balance_of(m_balances, to);
            if to == msg::sender { m_balances } else {
                m_balances
                    .update(msg::sender, bal_from - amount)
                    .update(to,          bal_to   + amount)
            }
        }
        in transferFrom(from_addr, to, amount) => {
            let bal_from = balance_of(m_balances, from_addr);
            let bal_to   = balance_of(m_balances, to);
            if to == from_addr { m_balances } else {
                m_balances
                    .update(from_addr, bal_from - amount)
                    .update(to,        bal_to   + amount)
            }
        }
    }

    // The share allowance covers two different spends and both go through
    // the same slot: a plain `transferFrom` of the share, and a `withdraw` or
    // `redeem` where the caller is not the owner. The second is the one
    // routers use, and forgetting to decrement it there is how a single
    // approval becomes an unlimited one.
    m_allowances: HashMap<U256, U256> {
        in approve(spender, amount) => {
            m_allowances.update(hashOf(msg::sender, spender), amount)
        }
        in transferFrom(from_addr, _, amount) => {
            let key  = hashOf(from_addr, msg::sender);
            let prev = allowance_of(m_allowances, from_addr, msg::sender);
            m_allowances.update(key, allowance_after_spend(prev, amount))
        }
        in withdraw(assets, _, owner) => settle: {
            let key   = hashOf(owner, msg::sender);
            let spend = ratio_shares_up(assets, m_total_supply, held);
            m_allowances.update(key,
                share_allowance_after_burn(m_allowances, owner,
                                           msg::sender, spend))
        }
        in redeem(shares, _, owner) => settle: {
            let key = hashOf(owner, msg::sender);
            m_allowances.update(key,
                share_allowance_after_burn(m_allowances, owner,
                                           msg::sender, shares))
        }
    }
}
